|> Section
    *The topic.*
    A

    __dimension__

    is a category of units such as “time”, as
    touched upon in the 
    [Postcript to Chapter >>slopes#decoy:2](>>units_vs_dimensions).
    The topic of

    __dimensional analysis__

    is that of analyzing the units within a mathematical 
    expression to uncover contradictions (pointing
    to upstream errors) and/or conversely to offer
    validation.

|> Section
    handle=_ch6_start_

    *Notation: function signatures.*
    If $f$ is a function from $\rr$ to $\rr$
    we write

    $$
    f : [\A] \ra [\B]
    $$

    to indicate that $f$ takes inputs in units A and returns
    outputs in units B. The ordered pair $(\A, \B)$, written
    “$[\A] \ra [\B]$”, is the

    __signature__

    of $f$.

    |> Example
        !! handle=dimensional_first_example

        If

        $$
        z : [\lbs] \ra [\pounds]
        $$

        then its inputs denote pounds, the measure of weight,
        and its outputs pounds _sterling_, the measure of wealth.

|> Section
    split_here=Rest

    *Dimensionless units.*
    We write $[1]$ for dimensionless units, as when a quantity
    is divided by a like-dimensioned quantity. Thus

    $$
    \large \cos : [1] \ra [1]
    $$

    takes a ratio of arc length to radius—the radian—and returns
    a ratio of adjacent length to hypotenuse*. Both are “length over length”.
    The outputs of sin, tan, etc., are dimensionless for the same reason.

    |> ImageLeft
        src=images/c6_radian_dimensionless_ratio_cloud_v2.svg

    |> Pause

    _*Note._ To be precise, these “lengths” are actually
    coordinate changes that may be negative—except for the
    hypotenuse-a.k.a.-radius, which is always positive—so they
    are “signed lengths”, or _displacements_, more accurately. 
    Likewise the radian carries a sign—the radian says “rotate by
    this multiple of the radius, with ‘$+$’ for counterclockwise and ‘$-$’
    for clockwise\*\*”.

    |> Pause

    _\*\*Second Note._ To get into the weeds—re: clockwise
    vs. counterclockwise—what counts as the “positive” direction
    of rotation is actually the direction _from the positive $x$
    axis to the positive $y$ axis_:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_true_counterclockwiseness_1.svg

    What we mean is that if it was our custom to draw the
    $y$ axis pointing downwards, but still draw the $x$ axis
    pointing rightward, the “positive” direction of rotation
    would be clockwise, not counterclockwise:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_true_counterclockwiseness_2.svg

    (This actually happens inside of SVG, the
    web standard for so-called “vector graphics”, in which the $y$
    axis points downwards. In an SVG file, 
    |> span
        class=code-cartouche
        rotate(10)
    \ rotates objects by 10$^\circ$ _clockwise_.)

    |> ImageRight
        src=images/c6_SVG_cloud.svg

|> Section
    *Restrictions imposed on signatures by function
    operations.*
    When we add two functions, the same input is passed
    to both functions:

    !! $$
    !! (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)
    !! $$
    
    |> Image
        src=images/c6_equal_inputs_v2.svg
        style=margin-bottom:-1.3em

    For the sum to make sense, $f$ and $g$ must have the same
    input units and the same output units:

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\A] &\ra [\B] \\
    g : [\A] &\ra [\B] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    We cannot add apples to oranges at the output end, either.

    By contrast, when multiplying two functions we can
    stomach the output units to be different since
    composite units such as “kilowatt-hour”, “worker-years”, etc,
    exist, and likewise when dividing. 
    The table gives the required component signatures and the
    resultant signature for each of the five main function operations:

    |> Image
        handle=table_1_1 <<Table 1.1>>
        src=tmp-images/c6_flowers_here_bob_v2.svg

    |> StarDivider
        style=margin-top:-0.3em

|> Section

    *The unary function operations.*
    Two unary function operations are _negation_ and _reciprocal_,
    written “$-\f$” and “$1/f$”, and defined here:

    |> Image
        handle=table_1_2 <<Table 1.2>>
        !! src=images/c6_flowers_here_two_more.svg
        src=tmp-images/c6_flowers_here_two_more_v3.svg

    |> StarDivider
        style=margin-top:-0.3em

    !! [*See [Chapter >>trigonometry#decoy:5 §>>trigonometry-derivatives#decoy:2](>>negation-of-a-function-first-mention).]

    !! $$
    !! (-f)(x) = -f(x)\qquad(1/f)(x) = 1/f(x)
    !! $$

|> Section
    _handle=derivative_signature
    *Differentiation.*
    As reviewed in [Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4](>>units_of_the_derivative)
    (see also Exercise >>_complete_the_missing_units_#decoy:6 of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4),
    differentiation divides the units of the output
    by the units of the input. 
    In terms of signatures,
    $$
    f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]
    $$
    if and only if
    $$
    f' : [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]
    $$
    for any units A, B.
    Conversely, multiply the derivative's output units by its
    input units to recover the original output units.

|> Section
    *Operators.*
    On a conceptual note,
    we can view differentiation
    as a function-that-takes-functions-and-outputs-functions,
    the “bigger fish” of the function world. As mathematicians
    use the term

    __operator__

    to denote a function that takes other functions as inputs,
    we therefore speak of the

    __differentiation operator__

    depicted here as an eye-of-Godzilla flying saucer: 

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_differentiation_operator_intro_v2.svg

    The unary operations in [Table 1.2](>>table_1_2) likewise take
    one function as input. An operator can also take two functions
    as inputs: function addition takes $f$ and $g$ and returns
    the single function $f + g$. Here it is as a two-input-tube-flying-saucer:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_addition_operator_standalone_v2.svg

    The two inputs to this operator are the functions $f$ and $g$.
    The resulting function $f + g$ still takes one number $x$ as input,
    returning $f(x) + g(x)$.

    Operators that take two inputs are called

    __binary__

    operators, as with the operations in Table 1.1.
    This figure summarizes how the operators act on signatures:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_operators_acting_on_signatures_v2.svg

    |> StarDivider
        style=margin-top:-0.3em

|> Section
    *The sum rule.*
    The _sum rule_ states that

    |> Boxed
        $$
        (f + g)' = f' + g'
        $$

    for all differentiable functions $f$, $g : \rr \ra \rr$.
    To “check the units”, both sides must impose the same signature
    constraints and yield the same final signature.

    On the left, $f + g$ requires $f$ and $g$ to have the same
    input and output units; differentiation adds no constraints.
    On the right, if $f : [\A] \ra [\B]$ and $g : [\C] \ra [\D]$,
    adding $f'$ and $g'$ requires

    $$
    \A = \C, \qquad \B/\A = \D/\C,
    $$

    hence $\B = \D$ as well: the same constraints!
    With $f, g : [\A] \ra [\B]$, we have

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    (f + g)' &: [\A] \ra [\tB/\tA] \\
    f' + g' &: [\A] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    The sum rule “checks out” insofar as units are concerned:
    nothing untoward has been found!

    |> Example
        handle=_ms_x1_x2_example_
        
        Let $x_1, x_2 : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}]$ be differentiable
        functions giving position in meters as a function of time in seconds.
        Then

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        (x_1 + x_2)' &: [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \\
        x_1' + x_2' &: [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35}
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        Here the sum rule says that

        __the velocity of a sum of two points
        is the sum of their velocities__

    |> Example
        handle=differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample

        If

        $$
        x_1, x_2 : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        are given by

        $$
        x_1(t) = |t| = -x_2(t)
        $$

        for all $t \in \rr$ (meaning that

        $$
        x_2(t) = -|t|
        $$

        in the case of $x_2$) then $$(x_1 + x_2)'$$ is defined
        at $t = 0$ but $$x_1' + x_2'$$ is not. One can best see this 
        directly from the graphs of
        $x_1$, $x_2$, $x_1'$, $x_2'$, etc:

        |> Image
            src=images/c6_all_those_x1x2_graphs.svg

        Thus

        $$
        (x_1 + x_2)' \ne x_1' + x_2'
        $$

        but the “reason”, precisely, is that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are not
        differentiable, as they
        are not differentiable, specifically, at $t = 0$.
        (But this is why we say “for differentiable $f$, $g$”
        when we present the sum rule, or other such rules.)
        !! —it is
        !! a necessary disclaimer to keep the statement accurate.)

    |> Pause

    _Postscript._ Even if $f$ and $g$ are not
    both differentiable it is still 
    true, however, that

    |> Boxed
        handle=postscript-on-x_0
        $$
        (f + g)'(x_0) = f'(x_0) + g'(x_0)
        $$

    at all points $x_0 \in \rr$ _such that $f$ and $g$
    are both differentiable at that specific $x_0$_—this gives
    us a “point by point” version of the sum rule—more granular!
    !! (But you can no longer claim that $(f + g)' = f' + g'$
    !! because the boxed equation above may fail for _some_ [other] $x_0$.)

|> Section

    *The “early afteroon product rule”.*
    Gottfried Leibniz, co-inventor of calculus,
    initially guessed that 

    |> Boxed
        $$
        (fg)' = f'g'
        $$

    !! [disclaimer: “for $f$, $g$ differentiable”]
    which is incorrect, though Leibniz also had the
    distinction of making and correcting this
    guess 
    !! mistake 
    in the span 
    of a single day,
    as we know from his notes,
    whence this equation's moniker.

    Both sides require the same input units for $f$ and $g$:

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\A] &\ra [\B] \\
    g : [\A] &\ra [\C] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    So far so good! But

    $$
    (fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
    $$
    |> ImageLeft
        src=images/c6_fg_prime_signature_cloud_v2.svg
        offset-x=0em

    whereas

    $$
    f'g' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA^2]
    $$
    |> ImageRight
        src=images/c6_f_prime_g_prime_signature_cloud_v2.svg
        offset-x=0em

    The rule cannot be correct: the two sides produce different
    units for the same functions $f$ and $g$.

    |> Example
        handle=_second_x1x2_example_

        With $x_1, x_2 : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}]$ as in
        Example >>_ms_x1_x2_example_,

        $$
        (x_1x_2)' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}^2/\te{s}]
        $$
        
        whereas

        $$
        x_1'x_2' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}^2/\te{s}^2]
        $$

        so there is no way these two quantities could
        ever be equal, in general. (For example,
        changing from counting time in seconds to
        counting it in hours, while correspondingly
        modifying $x_1$ and $x_2$ to reflect this
        change, would multiply $(x_1x_2)'$ by $3600$ but
        would multiply $x_1'x_2'$ by $3600^2$—so if by
        some fluke the early afternoon product rule
        held before this change it would certainly\*
        [\*assuming $(x_1x_2)' \ne 0$] not hold after,
        and therefore, the early afternoon product rule
        cannot hold in general.)

        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/c6_x1x2_3600_cloud.svg
            offset-x=1em

        |> Pause

        _Note on Example >>_second_x1x2_example_#decoy:0._
        $x_1'x_2'$ has the same signature as $(x_1x_2)''$, but

        $$
        (x_1x_2)'' = x_1'x_2'
        $$

        is still wrong. Dimensional analysis can debunk an equation;
        matching dimensions cannot prove it.

!! |> Section
!!     *The product rule.*
!!     The real “product rule”, valid for differentiable functions $f$ and $g$, is

!!     |> Boxed

!!         $$
!!         (fg)' = f'g + fg'
!!         $$

!!     as covered in Exercise >>_product_rule_#decoy:19 of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4.
!!     To start the dimensional analysis, we can
!!     easily see that both sides of the equation
!!     !! impose the constraint
!!     !! have sound units
!!     !! if and only if 
!!     !! $f$, $g$ have signatures
!!     !! of the form
!!     impose signatures of the form

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{aligned}
!!     f : [\A] &\ra [\B] \\
!!     g : [\A] &\ra [\C] \up{1.35}
!!     \end{aligned}
!!     $$

!!     on $f$ and $g$. 
!!     From there,
!!     !! in which case

!!     $$
!!     (fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
!!     $$

!!     while

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{gathered}
!!     !! f': [\A] \ra [\tB/\tA] \\
!!     !! g' : [\A] \ra [\tC/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
!!     f'g :  [\A] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \\
!!     fg' :  [\A] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
!!     !! f'g + fg' :  [\A] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
!!     \end{gathered}
!!     $$

!!     and

!!     $$
!!     f'g + fg' :  [\A] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
!!     $$

!!     so $f'g + fg'$ has the
!!     same signature as $(fg)'$, i.e., 
!!     this formula passes the “sniff test” of
!!     dimensional analysis.
!!     !! dimensional
!!     !! analysis gives its _imprimatur_ to this formula!
!!     !! (It “passes the sniff test” of dimensional analysis, put
!!     !! differently.)
!!     !! !! (Which is, indeed, the correct one.)

|> Section
    handle=chain_rule_discovery

    *The chain rule.*
    Can we use dimensional analysis to _guess_ a formula for
    $(f \circ g)'$? Our “lego pieces” are $f$, $g$, $f'$, $g'$.
    With the composition's input and output units matched (Table 1.1),
    their signatures are

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    f            :  [\tB] \ra [\tC] \\
    g            :  [\tA] \ra [\tB] \up{1.35} \\
    f'           :  [\tB] \ra [\tC/\tB] \up{1.35} \\
    g'           :  [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35}
    !! (f \circ g)' :  [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    Our target is $(f\circ g)' : [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tA]$.
    Since

    $$
    [\tC/\tA] = \left[{\tC\over \tB}\right]\cdot\left[{\tB\over\tA}\right],
    $$

    we might multiply $f'$ and $g'$. But their input units differ!
    Feeding $g$ into $f'$ fixes that: try

    $$
    (f'\circ g)g'.
    $$

    The signatures are

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f'\circ g &: [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tB] \\
    g' &: [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    Both accept A, and their output units multiply to $\tC/\tA$.

    |> ImageRight
        src=images/c6_chain_rule_units_cloud.svg
        offset-x=0em

    It turns out that this is the correct formula:

    |> Boxed
        $$
        (f \circ g)' = (f'\circ g)g'
        $$

    ...for all differentiable functions $f$, $g : \rr \ra \rr$,
    a formula known as the eponymous _chain rule_$\rt{0.1}$!!

    |> Example
        Say that a cute

        |> Image
            src=images/c6_solo_RAT.svg

        is running a fundraising race. Let $f$ give the money raised
        in dollars as a function of position in meters, and $g$ give
        the rat's position as a function of elapsed time in seconds:

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\te{m}] \ra [\te{\$}] \\
        g : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}] \up{1.35}
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        At $t_0$ seconds, the fundraising rate $(f\circ g)'(t_0)$ is in dollars per second.
        To obtain it, multiply the dollars-per-meter rate at the rat's
        position, $f'(g(t_0))$, by its meters-per-second velocity, $g'(t_0)$:

        $$
        (f\circ g)'(t_0) = f'(g(t_0))g'(t_0).
        $$

        |> Image
            width=530px
            src=images/c6_explained_rat_equation.svg

        |> Pause

        _Note._ This equation only requires $g$ to be differentiable
        at $t_0$ and $f$ to be differentiable at $g(t_0)$,
        similarly to the [postscript](>>postscript-on-x_0) following Example >>differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample,
        relative to the sum rule.
        !! similarly to the sum rule. (Cf. the 
        !! [note after Example >>differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample](>>postscript-on-x_0).)
        !! to the more granular version of the
        !! sum rule, [given after Example >>differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample](>>postscript-on-x_0).

        !! similarly to the 
        !! !! which is like we did for the sum rule in the 
        !! [“Postscript” after
        !! Example >>differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample](>>postscript-on-x_0).


    |> Example
        handle=cos_cos_chain_rule

        We have

        $$
        (\cos \circ \cos)' = ((-\sin) \circ \cos)(-\sin)
        $$
        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/c6_chain_rule_reminder_cloud.svg
            offset-x=2em

        by [the chain rule](>>chain_rule_discovery), since $\cos' = -\sin$.
        Cancelling the minus signs gives

        $$
        (\cos \circ \cos)' = (\sin \circ \cos)\sin,
        $$

        or, evaluated at $x$,

        $$
        (\cos \circ \cos)'(x) = \sin(\cos(x))\sin(x).
        $$

        |> ImageRight
            src=images/c6_cancelling_signs_cloud.svg

    |> Example
        handle=cos_cos_chain_rule_continued
        We have

        $$
        (\cos \circ \cos)'(2.5) = \sin(\cos(2.5))\cdot \sin(2.5) = -0.429\ldots
        $$

        by Example >>cos_cos_chain_rule,
        which
        looks visually compatible with the graph
        of $x \ra \cos(\cos(x))$ [nb: it should be the slope]:
        !! since
        !! $(\cos \circ \cos)'(2.5)$ is supposed to be the slope
        !! of $y = \cos(\cos x)$ at $x = 2.5$:

        |> Image
            src=images/c6_sin_of_sin_graph.svg

        |> StarDivider

        _Note on Example >>cos_cos_chain_rule_continued##_. 
        The values of $\cos(\cos(x))$ can be throught of as
        the $x$-coordinates of a certain horizontal windshield-wiper
        whose angle at time $t$ is $\cos(t)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/c6_windshield_wiper.svg

        Since $-1 \leq \cos(t) \leq 1$, the wiper's angle stays between
        $-1\Rad$ and $+1\Rad$ (about $\pm57.29^\circ$), so its $x$-coordinate ranges from

        $$
        \cos(\pm1) \approx 0.54 \quad\te{to}\quad \cos(0) = 1,
        $$

        producing the compressed sinusoid-like wave above.

|> Section
    !! *Appendix: radians _&_ the dimensionless plane.*
    *Epilogue: the dimensionless plane.*
    Imagine that we define the radian value of an
    angle as

    __length subtended by the angle on the unit circle__

    as opposed to

    __arc length over radius__

    where the latter is the more traditional definition.
    !! In the second case, 
    In the latter definition, 
    the dimensions of the radian are
    
    $$
    {\te{length}\over\te{length}} = \te{dimensionless}
    $$

    whereas under the first definition it sounds like
    the dimensions are simply “length”. BUT: “length”
    _in what plane_? The “original” plane in which the
    unit circle is embedded has dimensionless axes! And
    therefore _length itself_$\rt{0.2}$ in that plane is a dimensionless
    quantity, not the usual “physical” notion of length!
    So both cases assign radians to be a dimensionless 
    unit!
    Whoo-hoo!

    Relatedly, you may have seen axes labelled “time/s” instead
    of “time $[\te{s}]$”:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_dimensionless_plane_1_v1.svg
        style=margin-top:-0.5em

    Dividing time by the unit “s” takes out the units, leaving a pure
    number that fits in the dimensionless plane. For example, the
    coordinate $3$ means

    $$
    3 = {\te{time}\over\te{s}} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \te{time} = 3\te{s}.
    $$

    The coordinate is the pure number $3$; the time is $3$ seconds!
